export const chinaInfoPanel=`<section id="extra-panel" hidden aria-label="Extra info"><button id="chinese-romanization-open" class="korean-article-link" type="button" aria-haspopup="dialog" aria-controls="chinese-romanization"><strong>Chinese romanization</strong><span>Pinyin, older English spellings, and Taiwan’s place names.</span></button></section>`;
export const chinaRomanizationDialog=`<dialog id="chinese-romanization" class="korean-reader" aria-labelledby="chinese-romanization-title"><header class="korean-reader-header"><h2 id="chinese-romanization-title">Chinese romanization</h2><button id="chinese-romanization-close" type="button" aria-label="Close article">×</button></header><article class="korean-reader-body"><p>Romanization writes a language in Latin letters. Different systems can spell the same Chinese name differently: Beijing and Peking both represent 北京.</p>
<nav class="korean-article-contents" aria-label="Article sections"><button data-chinese-section="pinyin">Hanyu Pinyin</button><button data-chinese-section="initials">Consonants</button><button data-chinese-section="finals">Vowels and finals</button><button data-chinese-section="spelling">Spelling rules</button><button data-chinese-section="older">Older spellings</button><button data-chinese-section="taiwan">Taiwan</button><button data-chinese-section="names">Reading place names</button></nav>
<section id="chinese-pinyin"><h3>Hanyu Pinyin</h3>
<p>Hanyu Pinyin represents Standard Mandarin pronunciation. The United Nations adopted it for Chinese geographical names in 1977. It underlies names such as Beijing, Guangzhou, and Chongqing. <a href="https://arhiiv.eki.ee/wgrs/rom1_zh.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">UNGEGN: Chinese romanization</a>.</p>
<p>Pinyin letters follow a consistent set of pronunciation rules. Learning their sound values makes unfamiliar place names easier to read. <a href="https://www.ctcfl.ox.ac.uk/materials_pinyin-notes/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Oxford: pronunciation guide</a>.</p>
<p>Mandarin has four basic tones and a neutral tone. Tone marks distinguish syllables that otherwise have the same spelling. Map labels commonly omit these marks.</p>
<div class="korean-table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Tone</th><th>Example</th><th>Pitch</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>First</td><td>mā</td><td>High and level</td></tr><tr><td>Second</td><td>má</td><td>Rising</td></tr><tr><td>Third</td><td>mǎ</td><td>Low; falls and rises in isolation</td></tr><tr><td>Fourth</td><td>mà</td><td>Falling</td></tr><tr><td>Neutral</td><td>ma</td><td>Short and unstressed; pitch depends on context</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Connected speech changes tone patterns. Before a first, second, or fourth tone, the third tone stays low. Before another third tone, it changes to a rising tone. <a href="https://opentext.ku.edu/tingyiting/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">University of Kansas: tones and pronunciation</a>.</p>
<p>An apostrophe marks a syllable boundary: Xi’an separates <i>xi</i> and <i>an</i>, whereas <i>xian</i> is one syllable. The two dots in <i>ü</i> distinguish it from <i>u</i>, as in Lü. <a href="https://www.loc.gov/catdir/pinyin/difference.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Library of Congress: Pinyin spelling</a>.</p></section>
<section id="chinese-initials"><h3>The 21 initial consonants</h3>
<p>A Mandarin syllable has an optional initial consonant, a final, and a tone. In <i>jīng</i>, the initial is <i>j</i>, the final is <i>ing</i>, and the tone is first. The final contains the vowel and any following sounds.</p>
<p>The table groups all 21 consonant initials by how they are formed. <i>Zh</i>, <i>ch</i>, and <i>sh</i> each represent one consonant. The pairs <i>b/p</i>, <i>d/t</i>, and <i>g/k</i> contrast a small release of breath with a stronger puff, called aspiration.</p>
<div class="korean-table-wrap"><table id="chinese-initial-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Initials</th><th scope="col">How to form them</th><th scope="col">Place-name examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>b / p</td><td>Close both lips; p adds a stronger puff of air.</td><td>Běijīng · Píngdǐngshān</td></tr><tr><td>m / f</td><td>m sends air through the nose; f uses the lower lip and upper teeth.</td><td>Miányáng · Fúzhōu</td></tr><tr><td>d / t</td><td>Touch the tongue near the upper teeth; t adds aspiration.</td><td>Dàlián · Tiānjīn</td></tr><tr><td>n / l</td><td>n is nasal; l lets air pass around the sides of the tongue.</td><td>Nánjīng · Luòyáng</td></tr><tr><td>g / k / h</td><td>Use the back of the tongue; k is aspirated, and h has audible friction.</td><td>Guǎngzhōu · Kūnmíng · Hángzhōu</td></tr><tr><td>j / q / x</td><td>Raise the tongue toward the hard palate. j and q begin with closure; q adds aspiration. x has continuous friction.</td><td>Jǐnán · Qīngdǎo · Xī’ān</td></tr><tr><td>zh / ch / sh / r</td><td>Raise the tongue tip farther back. ch adds aspiration; sh has continuous friction, and r is voiced.</td><td>Zhūhǎi · Chángshā · Shànghǎi · Rìzhào</td></tr><tr><td>z / c / s</td><td>Keep the tongue forward. z and c begin with a brief closure; c adds aspiration. s has continuous friction.</td><td>Zūnyì · Cāngzhōu · Sūzhōu</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>The letters <i>y</i> and <i>w</i> organize vowel-initial syllables in writing, as in <i>yi</i> and <i>wu</i>. They sit outside this 21-consonant inventory. English sound comparisons give only an approximation; recorded examples help distinguish the three groups <i>j/q/x</i>, <i>zh/ch/sh</i>, and <i>z/c/s</i>. <a href="https://opentext.ku.edu/tingyiting/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">University of Kansas: consonant lessons and audio</a>; <a href="https://opentext.ku.edu/tingyiting/chapter/lesson9/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">sh and x compared</a>.</p></section>
<section id="chinese-finals"><h3>Vowels and finals</h3>
<p>The six basic vowel letters are <i>a, o, e, i, u, ü</i>. Their pronunciation depends on the surrounding sounds. To form <i>ü</i>, hold the tongue in the position for “ee” and round the lips.</p>
<div class="korean-table-wrap"><table id="chinese-final-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Finals</th><th scope="col">Reading guide</th><th scope="col">Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>a, o, e, i, u, ü</td><td>Basic vowel spellings; e and i have contextual readings.</td><td>Dàlǐ · Lǚliáng</td></tr><tr><td>ai, ei, ao, ou</td><td>The vowel moves toward another position within one syllable.</td><td>Táiběi · Bǎotóu</td></tr><tr><td>an, en, in, un, ün</td><td>End with the tongue touching near the upper teeth for n.</td><td>Tiānjīn</td></tr><tr><td>ang, eng, ing, ong</td><td>End with the back of the tongue raised for ng.</td><td>Shāndōng</td></tr><tr><td>ia, ie, iao, ian, iang, iong</td><td>Begin with a brief i-like glide.</td><td>Xiàmén · Jiāngsū</td></tr><tr><td>ua, uo, uai, uan, uang</td><td>Begin with a brief u-like glide.</td><td>Guǎngzhōu</td></tr><tr><td>üe, üan</td><td>Begin with a rounded, front-tongue vowel.</td><td>Yuè in Yuèyáng</td></tr><tr><td>er</td><td>A vowel with an r-like quality.</td><td>Ěrduōsī</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>These groups introduce common finals; the combinations permitted after each initial follow Mandarin syllable patterns. <a href="https://web.mit.edu/jinzhang/www/pinyin/finals/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MIT: finals and recorded examples</a>.</p>
<p>The ending <i>ng</i> is one nasal sound, like the end of “sing.” The endings distinguish syllables such as <i>xīn</i> and <i>xīng</i>. In <i>zi, ci, si, zhi, chi, shi, ri</i>, the written <i>i</i> extends the preceding tongue position. These syllables have a different vowel quality from <i>mi</i> or <i>li</i>. <a href="https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/res-21g-003-learning-chinese-a-foundation-course-in-mandarin-spring-2011/01af9a25b804b7d58a26fa580691df4f_MITRES_21G_003S11_pinyin.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MIT: syllables and pronunciation</a>.</p></section>
<section id="chinese-spelling"><h3>Spelling rules that affect names</h3>
<div class="korean-table-wrap"><table id="chinese-spelling-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Rule</th><th scope="col">Example</th><th scope="col">What to notice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Shortened finals</td><td>Liǔzhōu · Guìlín · Kūnmíng</td><td>After an initial, iou, uei, and uen shorten to iu, ui, and un.</td></tr><tr><td>Vowel-initial syllables</td><td>Yīchāng · Wǔhàn</td><td>Pinyin uses y or w to mark syllables beginning with i- or u-type sounds.</td></tr><tr><td>The hidden ü</td><td>Yùlín · Quánzhōu</td><td>After j, q, x, and y, the dots on ü are omitted.</td></tr><tr><td>The visible ü</td><td>Lǚliáng</td><td>After n and l, the dots distinguish ü from u.</td></tr><tr><td>Syllable boundaries</td><td>Xī’ān · Tiān’ān</td><td>An apostrophe separates syllables where the next begins with a, o, or e.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Tone marks go above a vowel: <i>a</i> takes priority, followed by <i>o</i> or <i>e</i>. In <i>iu</i> and <i>ui</i>, the mark goes above the second vowel, as in <i>liǔ</i> and <i>guì</i>. <a href="https://www.ctcfl.ox.ac.uk/materials_pinyin-notes/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Oxford: written forms and tones</a>.</p>
<p>Pinyin joins the syllables within a place name: <i>Bei + jing</i> becomes <i>Beijing</i>. An administrative term can follow separately, as in <i>Beijing Shi</i>. English map labels can translate that term or omit it. <a href="https://www.loc.gov/catdir/pinyin/difference.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Library of Congress: word division</a>.</p></section>
<section id="chinese-older"><h3>Wade–Giles and older English names</h3>
<p>Wade–Giles is an older system for Mandarin. Its apostrophe marks aspiration: a stronger release of breath after a consonant. Pinyin represents that distinction with different letters.</p>
<div class="korean-table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Wade–Giles</th><th>Pinyin</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>p / p’</td><td>b / p</td></tr><tr><td>t / t’</td><td>d / t</td></tr><tr><td>k / k’</td><td>g / k</td></tr><tr><td>hs</td><td>x</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Other correspondences depend on the following vowel. The Chinese characters help establish the correct reading. <a href="https://arhiiv.eki.ee/wgrs/rom1_zh.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">UNGEGN: Wade–Giles comparison</a>.</p>
<p>Older English names also reflect postal conventions and pronunciations beyond Standard Mandarin. These names come from several spelling traditions. In pairs such as Peking and Beijing, the Chinese name remains the same while its English spelling changes.</p>
<div class="korean-table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Older English spelling</th><th>Current atlas name</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Peking</td><td>Beijing</td></tr><tr><td>Canton</td><td>Guangzhou</td></tr><tr><td>Nanking</td><td>Nanjing</td></tr><tr><td>Tientsin</td><td>Tianjin</td></tr><tr><td>Chungking</td><td>Chongqing</td></tr><tr><td>Amoy</td><td>Xiamen</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Search accepts these older spellings, along with names such as Tsingtao, Foochow, Ningpo, Soochow, Hangchow, and Chengtu. <a href="https://www.loc.gov/catdir/pinyin/history.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Library of Congress: geographical names</a>; <a href="https://visualizingcultures.mit.edu/opium_wars_01/pinyin.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MIT: historical spelling conventions</a>.</p></section>
<section id="chinese-taiwan"><h3>Taiwan’s established names</h3>
<p>Taiwan’s county and city names retain established English spellings, including Taipei, Chiayi, and Kaohsiung. These forms coexist with Hanyu Pinyin and other romanization systems. Taiwan’s official place-name dataset records alternative transliterations. <a href="https://data.gov.tw/en/datasets/7064" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ministry of the Interior: place-name transliterations</a>.</p>
<div class="korean-table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Atlas spelling</th><th>Hanyu Pinyin</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Taipei</td><td>Taibei</td></tr><tr><td>Chiayi</td><td>Jiayi</td></tr><tr><td>Kaohsiung</td><td>Gaoxiong</td></tr><tr><td>Hsinchu</td><td>Xinzhu</td></tr><tr><td>Keelung</td><td>Jilong</td></tr><tr><td>Kinmen</td><td>Jinmen</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Search accepts Hanyu Pinyin for all 22 county and city divisions. Their established English names remain on the map. Tone marks and spaces are optional. Jiayi finds both Chiayi City and Chiayi County; Xinzhu finds both Hsinchu jurisdictions.</p>
<p>Pinyin represents Standard Mandarin. Names derived from Tibetan, Uyghur, Mongolian, and other languages require their own spelling conventions. <a href="https://arhiiv.eki.ee/wgrs/rom1_zh.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">UNGEGN: names in minority languages</a>.</p></section><section id="chinese-names"><h3>Reading place names</h3>
<p>Recurring characters give clues to a name’s meaning. <i>Běi</i> 北 means north, <i>nán</i> 南 south, <i>dōng</i> 东 east, and <i>xī</i> 西 west. Beijing and Nanjing share <i>jīng</i> 京, “capital”: their names mean northern capital and southern capital.</p>
<p>Administrative terms describe the jurisdiction. <i>Shěng</i> 省 means province, <i>shì</i> 市 city, <i>xiàn</i> 县 county, and <i>qū</i> 区 district. A city’s administrative territory can include rural areas and subordinate towns. Here, “city” identifies an administrative jurisdiction.</p>
<p>Characters with several readings require care in names. In Chongqing, 重 is read <i>chóng</i>; in Xiamen, 厦 is read <i>xià</i>. The place name determines the reading. Chinese characters and the parent region also help distinguish places whose romanized names look alike. <a href="https://www.loc.gov/catdir/pinyin/history.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Library of Congress: Chinese geographical names</a>.</p>
<p>For pronunciation practice, the <a href="https://www.uvm.edu/~chinese/pinyin.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">University of Vermont’s syllable tables</a> provide recorded combinations. The <a href="https://opentext.ku.edu/tingyiting/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">University of Kansas course</a> adds listening exercises and lessons on tone changes.</p></section></article></dialog>`;
export function wireChinaInfo(){
 const dialog=document.getElementById('chinese-romanization');
 document.getElementById('chinese-romanization-open').onclick=()=>dialog.showModal();
 document.getElementById('chinese-romanization-close').onclick=()=>dialog.close();
 dialog.addEventListener('click',event=>{if(event.target===dialog){const r=dialog.getBoundingClientRect();if(event.clientX<r.left||event.clientX>r.right||event.clientY<r.top||event.clientY>r.bottom)dialog.close();}});
 for(const button of dialog.querySelectorAll('[data-chinese-section]'))button.onclick=()=>{const section=document.getElementById('chinese-'+button.dataset.chineseSection);section.scrollIntoView({block:'start',behavior:'instant'});const heading=section.querySelector('h3');heading.tabIndex=-1;heading.focus({preventScroll:true});};
 dialog.addEventListener('close',()=>{dialog.querySelector('article').scrollTop=0;});
 return {close(){if(dialog.open)dialog.close();}};
}
